# App Router Module Rules

## Purpose
Next.js 16 App Router pages, layouts, and route handlers. This includes authentication pages, marketing pages, dashboard pages, and layouts.

## Entry Points
- `src/app/page.tsx` - Root page
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Root layout
- `src/app/(auth)/**` - Authentication routes
- `src/app/(marketing)/**` - Public marketing pages
- `src/app/dashboard/**` - Protected dashboard routes

## Allowed Imports
- `@/components/*` - React components
- `@/lib/*` - Core utilities (db, auth, utils)
- `@/utils/*` - Helper functions
- Next.js built-ins: `next/server`, `next/navigation`, `next/link`, `next/image`
- React and React DOM

## Forbidden Imports
- Direct database client imports (use `@/lib/db` instead)
- Direct API route imports (use fetch or TanStack Query)
- Node.js built-ins (use Next.js equivalents)

## Coding Conventions

### Server Components (Default)
- Use Server Components by default
- Server Components can be async for data fetching
- No `"use client"` directive unless needed

### Client Components
- Add `"use client"` directive only when needed:
  - Using React hooks (useState, useEffect, etc.)
  - Event handlers (onClick, onChange, etc.)
  - Browser APIs (localStorage, window, etc.)
  - Context providers

### Data Fetching
- Use async Server Components for server-side data fetching
- Use TanStack Query for client-side data fetching
- Use `@/lib/db` for database queries in Server Components

### Route Protection
- Protected routes are handled by `middleware.ts`
- Dashboard routes require authentication (checked in middleware)
- Use `auth.api.getSession()` for session checks

### Page Structure
```typescript
// Example: Server Component page
export default async function MyPage() {
  const data = await fetchData();
  return <div>{data.title}</div>;
}
```

### Layout Structure
```typescript
// Example: Layout with providers
export default function Layout({ children }) {
  return (
    <Provider>
      {children}
    </Provider>
  );
}
```

## AI Hints
- Default to Server Components - only add `"use client"` when absolutely necessary
- Use async Server Components for data fetching
- Leverage Next.js 16 App Router features (parallel routes, intercepting routes)
- Keep pages focused and single-purpose
- Use route groups `(auth)`, `(marketing)` for organization
